{
  "statement": "AI-generated prototype code inside a 90-day MVP sprint is not a speed advantage. It is a cost-deferral mechanism. The code ships fast, the launch looks great, and then quarters two through eight arrive with a maintenance bill nobody budgeted for. If you are a technology executive evaluating whether to let your team lean on AI code generation to hit a 90-day window, the question is not \"how fast can we ship?\" The question is: who pays for the speed, and when?\n\nHere is the core problem. AI tools can generate thousands of lines of code in an afternoon. Your engineering team can deeply comprehend maybe a fraction of that output under sprint pressure. The rest is not an asset — it is a liability with a bow on it. Software engineering research consistently shows that modifying code without original-author knowledge costs 3 to 5 times more than modifying code the team wrote and understands. In a 90-day sprint, engineers accept AI output without deep review because the deadline demands it. They ship code they did not write and do not fully understand. The institutional knowledge of why the code is structured the way it is — the reasoning, the tradeoffs, the edge cases — never enters anyone's head. It exists nowhere except in the model's opaque generation process. When requirements change — and in consumer software, 40 to 60 percent of MVP features are modified or removed within six months — the team cannot safely evolve what it cannot explain. That is not technical debt. That is a knowledge void, and it is the mechanism that converts your 90-day sprint into a multi-quarter remediation project.\n\nNow look at the incentive structure across Product, Engineering, and Finance. Product measures feature velocity. They celebrate on launch day and move to the next initiative. Finance measures upfront cost-per-feature and sees AI-generated code as a bargain — fewer engineer-hours per feature shipped. Neither metric captures what happens next. Engineering inherits a codebase that no human authored, that no human deeply reviewed, and that the AI tool may not reproduce identically if asked again. The business case for rewriting working code never materializes. Product has moved on. Finance sees no ROI in replacing something that already ships. This is the proof-of-concept trap, and it is not speculation — it is the documented lifecycle of every rapid-prototyping wave from 4GL tools in the 1980s through the low-code platforms of the 2010s. Pilot code that \"works\" gets promoted to production without the investment needed to make it maintainable. The competing incentives do not accidentally produce this outcome. They structurally guarantee it.\n\nThe productivity studies will come up. Someone will cite GitHub's claim that Copilot increases developer task completion speed by 55 percent. That number measures short-term output volume on isolated tasks. It does not measure whether the team understands the output. It does not measure defect rates at month six. It does not capture the uncompensated verification labor — engineers spending hours reviewing, debugging, and mentally modeling AI-generated code that gets booked as \"AI productivity\" rather than human cost. It does not capture the opportunity cost of engineers learning to manage AI output instead of building deep domain expertise in the product's architecture. And it certainly does not capture the compounding cost of technical debt that was never understood well enough to be catalogued. Empirical research on technical debt economics shows deferred quality costs compound at 20 to 40 percent annually. Your 90-day savings become your 18-month budget crater.\n\nEvery previous generation of \"rapid development\" tooling promised the same acceleration and delivered the same pattern: fast initial output, slow organizational integration, expensive long-term maintenance. AI code generation is a normal tool subject to normal integration constraints. The binding constraint on your MVP is not how fast the AI can generate code. It is how fast your organization can metabolize that code — understand it, test it, document it, and prepare to evolve it. If you ship faster than your team can absorb, you are not accelerating validated learning. You are manufacturing ignorance at scale. So before anyone signs off on the 90-day plan, ask Finance one question: what line item covers the rewrite that Engineering will need in quarter three, and who approved it?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧊", "tooltip": "AI-generated code is an iceberg — the visible speed is small compared to the hidden maintenance mass below the waterline."},
    {"symbol": "🏚️", "tooltip": "Prototype code becomes permanent infrastructure the way a temporary building becomes a permanent office — nobody funds the replacement."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "The 90-day sprint is an hourglass — the sand you save now piles up as debt on the other side."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "AI-generated prototype code inside a 90-day MVP sprint is not a speed advantage — it is a cost-deferral mechanism.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Modifying code without original-author knowledge costs 3 to 5 times more than modifying code the team wrote and understands.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "In consumer software, 40 to 60 percent of MVP features are modified or removed within six months.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Deferred quality costs compound at 20 to 40 percent annually, turning 90-day savings into an 18-month budget crater.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "The binding constraint on your MVP is not how fast the AI can generate code — it is how fast your organization can metabolize that code.", "targets": []}
  ]
}

