t and attrition six months later.\n\nThe 90-day timeline makes this worse through a mechanism that has nothing to do with technology. Call it the prototype trap. Product declares victory at launch. Finance locks in the cost baseline and reports the savings. Engineering is left holding a codebase that was never designed to survive contact with real users at scale — but now it must, because there is no scheduled moment to replace it. This pattern predates AI tools entirely. Industry data on AI pilot projects shows the vast majority stall at the proof-of-concept stage because organizations lack the integration infrastructure and strategic roadmaps to graduate prototype code into production-grade systems. An established company is actually more vulnerable to this trap than a startup, not less. Startups can throw away an MVP and rebuild. An established company has existing systems to integrate with, existing quality standards to meet, existing users who will judge a buggy release against the company's track record. The organizational incentives are perfectly aligned to prevent the refactoring from ever happening: Product has moved on to the next feature, Finance has already booked the savings, and Engineering lacks the political capital to demand a rewrite of code that \"already works.\"\n\nFinance's cost-per-feature calculation for AI-generated MVPs is missing at least three line items. First: technical debt servicing. Every dollar of shortcut during initial development costs between $3 and $10 in future maintenance — that ratio is well-established in software economics literature and AI-generated code doesn't improve it, it just hides the shortcuts deeper. Second: security audit costs for code whose provenance and logic nobody on the team can fully trace. Third: the opportunity cost of engineers spending future sprints remediating AI-generated code instead of building new features. None of these appear in the 90-day budget because the entire point of the 90-day sprint 