{
  "statement": "The scarce resource in a 90-day consumer MVP is not code quality. It's market learning. Every week your team spends hand-crafting prototype code that has a 40-60% chance of being thrown away is a week you're not learning what users actually want. AI-generated prototype code compresses time-to-insight. That's not a side benefit — it's the entire point of an MVP. Instagram didn't win because its first codebase was pristine. It won because it shipped, watched users, and iterated faster than competitors. Validated learning comes from instrumentation — analytics, A/B tests, funnel metrics, session recordings. It does not come from reading source code. If your telemetry is right, the authorship of the code underneath is irrelevant to the learning loop.\n\nThe Skeptic's strongest argument is that unfamiliar code costs 3-5x more to modify, and that AI-generated code is permanently unfamiliar because no one on the team authored it. That's a real friction. But here's what it misses: the comparison class is wrong. In any 90-day sprint, human-authored code also becomes unfamiliar to teammates who didn't write it. The 3-5x multiplier is a general software engineering finding about all prototype code under time pressure, not a special property of AI authorship. The honest comparison is AI-generated prototype versus human-authored prototype under the same 90-day constraint. Under that comparison, AI-generated code arrives faster — often 2-3x faster for boilerplate, scaffolding, and CRUD layers. That speed dividend buys engineering hours to invest in the things that actually reduce the modification multiplier: test coverage, inline documentation, architectural decision records. Comprehensibility isn't an inherent property of who wrote the code. It's a measurable variable — onboarding-time-to-productive-contribution — and it responds to targeted investment.\n\nThe Skeptic's compounding model — 20-40% annual growth in deferred quality costs — assumes the prototype code persists unchanged indefinitely. That assumption describes organizational dysfunction, not inevitability. An established consumer software company has CI/CD pipelines, automated test suites, code review processes, and senior engineers who can impose structure on AI-generated modules. They have the capacity to plan and execute a v2 rewrite — something a cash-strapped startup cannot do. The Skeptic's own claim that \"the binding constraint is organizational metabolization speed\" actually favors established teams. These teams metabolize faster. They can scope the AI-generated code into disposable modules behind feature flags, run the experiment, and replace the prototype with production-grade code once validated demand justifies the investment. The compounding window isn't years. It's months — bounded by the team's own release cadence.\n\nProduct, Engineering, and Finance look like they're pulling in three directions. Product wants feature velocity. Engineering wants code stability. Finance wants growth per dollar. But for a low-risk consumer product, all three share the same enemy: building to unvalidated assumptions. Product gets features in front of real users to test. Engineering gets actual usage data to inform architecture decisions for v2 instead of guessing at requirements in a vacuum. Finance gets a validated demand signal before committing production-grade engineering spend — that's capital efficiency by definition. The tension only turns destructive when the organization treats prototype code as production code. That's a planning discipline problem, not an AI problem. The real financial waste is paying senior engineers $180K+ per year to carefully hand-author features that have a coin-flip chance of surviving six months.\n\nThe Skeptic is accounting for technical debt and ignoring learning debt. Not shipping has its own compounding cost: competitor entry, user preference drift, market window closure, and engineering time wasted building to assumptions that turn out to be wrong. For consumer software, those costs compound faster than code maintenance costs. Ask the teams at Google who shipped Google+ eighteen months late with beautiful architecture — and lost the social market entirely. The question isn't whether AI-generated prototype code creates maintenance burden. Of course it does. The question is whether that bounded, manageable burden is worth the 4-6 weeks of compressed learning it buys you. For an established company with the infrastructure to metabolize and replace that code on a planned schedule, the answer is not close.",
