nvestment in quality infrastructure for AI-generated code is conservative, not aspirational. Finance gets a predictable cost model with the maintenance line item visible from day one. Engineering gets explicit authority and budget for remediation. Product keeps velocity. The Skeptic asks who approved the rewrite budget. The answer: we did, before the sprint started.\n\nValidated learning requires live users generating real behavior data. You cannot A/B test a feature that never shipped. You cannot measure conversion lift on a prototype sitting in a staging environment. For a low-risk consumer product where the failure ceiling is buggy releases and increased support tickets — not safety incidents or regulatory exposure — the learning value of shipping on day 85 with AI-generated code and a planned refactoring budget dwarfs the learning value of shipping on day 140 with hand-authored code that nobody will rewrite anyway because the next feature sprint already started. The question for any technology executive is not whether AI-generated code creates maintenance cost. Of course it does. All code does. The question is whether the competitive position, user feedback, and validated learning gained by shipping on time justify that cost. For an established consumer software company with verification infrastructure and a pre-budgeted remediation plan, the answer is yes — and the cost of pretending otherwise is measured in lost market windows that no amount of clean code can buy back.",
