ied to the meta-layer — trusting the machine to diagnose its own mistakes.\n\nThe threshold debate reveals a deeper problem. The proposed trip wires — 40% remediation sprint raiding, 30% MTTR increase over 90 days — are autopsies, not health checks. By the time MTTR has degraded 30% between sprint 1 and sprint 6, your team has spent 12 weeks accepting AI output without building independent mental models. The cognitive offloading is already entrenched. \"AI In The Book Publishing Industry Statistics 2026\" found 61% of editors reported skill atrophy from AI over-reliance — and that's in a domain with far slower feedback loops than software shipping. The leading indicators matter more: when regeneration dependency exceeds 30% of modification events, engineers are no longer editing code, they're re-rolling the dice. When fix-attempt churn exceeds 3 attempts per incident, the team is guessing rather than diagnosing. When diagnostic latency ratio exceeds 2:1 compared to human-written module baselines, comprehension debt is compounding in real time. These fire weeks before MTTR degrades. Accepting the lagging thresholds without the leading indicators is like monitoring a patient's heart rate but not their blood pressure — you'll know when they collapse, but you won't catch the stroke building.\n\nSo here's the operational question nobody wants to answer: when the leading indicators fire at week 4 — regeneration dependency at 35%, diagnostic latency ratio at 2.3:1 — who on the team has the authority to slow down AI-generated code production, and what happens to them when Product leadership says the launch date hasn't moved?",
