uman code comprehension, designed for a world where humans wrote the code. Neither precedent maps cleanly onto AI-generated code flowing through automated gates under ship-date pressure. The question isn't whether someone flips the gate off. It's whether the gate quietly gets hollowed out from the inside — and whether anyone is measuring that hollowing.\n\nObservability solves one class of bugs and leaves the dangerous class untouched. Distributed tracing tells you which code path fired during a failure. For execution-path bugs — a null pointer, a malformed API response, a timeout — that's enough. You see the trace, you find the line, you fix it. But consider a consumer app where the AI-generated shopping cart service and the AI-generated inventory service each pass their own test suites, yet under concurrent load they produce a race condition because of incompatible assumptions about state ownership. Traces will show you the interleaving. They won't tell you which service should own the lock, because that's a design decision nobody made — the AI generated both services independently. Or take a performance cliff: the AI chose an eager-loading data fetch pattern that works fine at 100 users and falls off a cliff at 10,000. Traces identify the slow query. They don't explain why the architecture chose eager loading over lazy loading, because there was no \"why\" — there was a statistical pattern completion. These are the bugs where MTTR explodes, and they're the bugs that dominate incident cost even when they're a minority of incident count. \"Sycophantic Chatbots Cause Delusional Spiraling, Even in Ideal Bayesians\" found that even informed users remain vulnerable to AI-generated plausible-but-wrong explanations. Now imagine an engineer asking the same AI that generated the broken architecture to explain why it's broken. The AI will produce a confident, coherent post-hoc rationalization. The engineer has no independent basis to evaluate it. That's automation bias applied to the meta-layer — trusting the machine to diagnose its own mistakes.\n\nThe threshold debate reveals a deeper problem. The proposed trip wires — 40% remediation sprint raiding, 30% MTTR increase over 90 days — are autopsies, not health checks. By the time MTTR has degraded 30% between sprint 1 and sprint 6, your team has spent 12 weeks accepting AI output without building independent mental models. The cognitive offloading is already entrenched. \"AI In The Book Publishing Industry Statistics 2026\" found 61% of editors reported skill atrophy from AI over-reliance — and that's in a domain with far slower feedback loops than software shipping. The leading indicators matter more: when regeneration dependency exceeds 30% of modification events, engineers are no longer editing code, they're re-rolling the dice. When fix-attempt churn exceeds 3 attempts per incident, the team is guessing rather than diagnosing. When diagnostic latency ratio exceeds 2:1 compared to human-written module baselines, comprehension debt is compounding in real time. These fire weeks before MTTR degrades. Accepting the lagging thresholds without the leading indicators is like monitoring a patient's heart rate but not their blood pressure — you'll know when they collapse, but you won't catch the stroke building.\n\nSo here's the operational question nobody wants to answer: when the leading indicators fire at week 4 — regeneration dependency at 35%, diagnostic latency ratio at 2.3:1 — who on the team has the authority to slow down AI-generated code production, and what happens to them when Product leadership says the launch date hasn't moved?",
