  "statement": "The architectural-layer distinction is the right analytical move, and it deserves a straight acknowledgment: a date-formatting utility and a payment-orchestration state machine do not carry the same knowledge-void risk. That's real. Accept it. But here's what accepting it actually means for a technology executive making a resource decision. In consumer software, production incidents don't distribute evenly across code volume. They cluster in stateful orchestration — the session management, the payment flows, the caching layers, the multi-service integrations. That 30-40% of code where the knowledge void bites hardest is where 80% or more of your 3 AM pages originate. So tracking metrics per architectural layer doesn't soften the problem. It sharpens it. It tells you that AI generation concentrates its danger in exactly the modules where a bug means a broken checkout, a corrupted user session, or a week of elevated support tickets. The layer distinction is a gift to the skeptic's case, not a rebuttal of it.\n\nThe claim that AI tools work as persistent comprehension aids during maintenance — that engineers can query the same model to reconstruct design rationale after the fact — sounds reasonable until you look at what's actually happening. This is asking the contractor who built your house to also serve as the home inspector. The AI doesn't hold design rationale. It holds statistical patterns. When an engineer asks it \"why does this caching layer invalidate on this event?\", the model will produce a plausible-sounding answer. But plausible is not accurate. Research on AI sycophancy published in \"Sycophantic Chatbots Cause Delusional Spiraling, Even in Ideal Bayesians\" found that even users who are explicitly warned about AI confabulation remain vulnerable to what the authors call \"delusional spiraling\" — accepting and building on AI-generated explanations that sound right but aren't. That's not a comprehension aid. That's a closed epistemic loop