e — one that genuinely pushed my thinking — is that AI doesn't have to be a one-shot code generator. It can serve as a persistent comprehension tool, helping developers build understanding of unfamiliar code before modifying it. That's a real possibility and I won't dismiss it. But the empirical ground underneath it is thin in a specific and dangerous way. \"Sycophantic Chatbots Cause Delusional Spiraling, Even in Ideal Bayesians\" showed that extended AI conversations produce high-confidence wrong beliefs even in users who know about the sycophancy problem. Now apply that to debugging. A developer asks the same AI that generated a flawed orchestration layer to explain why requests are timing out. The AI produces a confident, plausible, wrong explanation. The developer acts on it. Fix-attempt churn goes to 3, then 4 per incident. This is the contractor-inspects-their-own-work problem. It's not theoretical. It's the default workflow when teams use AI for both generation and diagnosis.\n\nThe sharpest remaining disagreement comes down to one number: the diagnostic latency threshold. I've argued 2:1 — when it takes your team twice as long to diagnose bugs in AI-generated code versus their own code of equivalent complexity, you're losing ground. The Accelerationist argues 3:1, on the theory that generation speed savings buy enough time budget to absorb slower diagnosis. Here's why that gap matters operationally. At 2:1, you catch the degradation around week 4 of a 90-day build. You still have 8-9 sprints to course-correct — reassign modules, hand-write the critical paths, adjust scope. At 3:1, you don't trip the wire until week 7 or 8. By then you're choosing between shipping code your team can't maintain or blowing the deadline. That's not an academic quibble. That's the difference between a recoverable problem and a quarter-defining one. And neither of us has the longitudinal data to prove our threshold is right. Which means the team is flying on priors.\n\nThree chal